WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:06.540
MIT wunderkind Riri Williams is in Namor's sights&nbsp;
in "Black Panther: Wakanda Forever," but would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:11.220
killing the brilliant student actually achieve&nbsp;
his goals, or just be another needless death?

00:00:11.522 --> 00:00:12.701
Warning: Spoilers ahead.

00:00:13.109 --> 00:00:17.460
Namor is one of the oldest characters in&nbsp;
Marvel Comics. He's been a hero, a villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:21.540
and pretty much everything in between throughout&nbsp;
that time, so it only makes sense for him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.440
have shades of gray in his big-screen&nbsp;
debut, "Black Panther: Wakanda Forever."

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:30.120
In the film, Namor is the ruler of Talokan,&nbsp;
an underwater civilization that works hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.240
to keep its presence a secret from&nbsp;
the rest of the world. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:36.960
things go awry when the United States&nbsp;
begins drilling for vibranium in the ocean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.300
courtesy of a machine originally designed by&nbsp;
Riri Williams. As a result, Namor enlists the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.800
help of Wakanda to track down Riri to bring&nbsp;
her to him so that Talokan can dispose of her.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:50.834
"I need to know if Wakanda&nbsp;
is an ally or an enemy."

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.920
Of course, upon meeting Riri, Shuri and&nbsp;
Okoye realize they can't merely hand her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.540
over to be killed by Namor, and what ensues&nbsp;
is a power struggle between the two nations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.480
A lot of people needlessly&nbsp;
die because of the conflict,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.980
and it all could've been avoided if Namor had&nbsp;
thought about his plan just a little more.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:12.180
The Talokan people attack the U.S.&nbsp;
government's vibranium drilling outpost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:17.700
disposing of everyone onboard either through a&nbsp;
siren song that makes them drown themselves or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.980
by combat. But that's not enough for Namor; he&nbsp;
wants the Wakandans to track down the scientist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:27.480
responsible for developing the technology and&nbsp;
deliver them to him so that he can kill her.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:32.220
The main problem with this plan is that it&nbsp;
does nothing to solve the issue currently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:36.840
facing Talokan. Namor doesn't want anyone&nbsp;
to know about the empire's existence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:42.420
and killing Riri does nothing to prevent that&nbsp;
from happening. Whether Riri lives or dies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:46.860
the technology is still out there, and it's&nbsp;
always possible for the government to chalk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:52.560
up the one attack to a fluke and build another&nbsp;
outpost to drill for more vibranium. In doing so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.240
they may inadvertently stumble upon Talokan's&nbsp;
existence, and Riri's death will have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:02.340
for nothing. Sure, she may be the only one who&nbsp;
understands the tech at the start of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:05.580
but by the time Namor's trying to&nbsp;
kill her, the cat is out of the bag.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:10.020
All Namor would be doing, then, is getting&nbsp;
revenge on someone who inadvertently put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:14.700
his people in harm's way. But then again, that&nbsp;
does seem to be part of Namor's modus operandi.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.420
Namor's plan may fall apart under scrutiny&nbsp;
for more than a couple of minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:22.200
but his desire to kill Riri at&nbsp;
least informs viewers about his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:26.040
character. In both the comics and&nbsp;
the movie, he's a protective ruler.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.960
"Namor loves his&nbsp;people. 

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:34.500
He's going to protect them, because&nbsp;
to be a ruler, you have to serve the people."

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:38.940
He will do anything to keep his people's&nbsp;
existence a secret from the rest of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:43.980
world. Killing Riri may not actually solve his&nbsp;
problems, but it would make himself feel better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:48.480
to get revenge on someone who threatened his&nbsp;
people, whether it was done intentionally or not.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.100
To look at it from Namor's perspective&nbsp;
for a second, if Riri is still out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:57.960
what's to stop her from inventing something else&nbsp;
that could potentially enable outsiders to locate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:02.160
Talokan? Or she could continue improving&nbsp;
the technology she designed initially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.000
causing even more countries to&nbsp;
drill for vibranium. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.140
Namor is completely unaware that Riri&nbsp;
isn't directly working with the government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:15.300
so in his mind, this is just another lackey&nbsp;
who poses a direct threat to his kingdom.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:19.980
Namor does what he thinks would be best for&nbsp;
his people, but perhaps if he had thought it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:24.360
through more carefully, he could've saved&nbsp;
both Talokan and Wakanda a lot of trouble.

